'''arch'''<sup>1</sup> (ärch), ''v.'' [< ''arch''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To cover with a vault, or span with an arch.
{{smaller block|The proud river . . . is ''arched'' over with . . . a curious pile of stones.
{{right|''Howell.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>No bridge ''arched'' thy waters save that where the trees
Stretched their long arms above thee and kissed in the breeze.</poem>
{{right|''Whittier'', Bridal of Pennacook.}}}}
'''2.''' To throw into the shape of an arch or vault; curve: as, the horse ''arches'' his neck.
{{smaller block|Fine devices of ''arching'' water without spilling.
{{right|''Bacon'', Gardens.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Beneath our keel the great sky ''arched''
Its liquid light and azure.</poem>
{{right|''H. P. Spofford'', Poems, p. 11.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To form an arch or arches: as,

